      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Certainly! Let's solve this step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the median:**\n   The set given is \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\). By definition, the median of a set with an odd number of elements is the middle element when the set is arranged in ascending order. The middle element is \\(n + 6\\).\n\n2. **Set up the equation for the median:**\n   According to the problem, the median is 9. Therefore, we can set up the following equation:\n   \\[\n   n + 6 = 9\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve for \\(n\\):**\n   \\[\n   n + 6 = 9\n   \\]\n   Subtract 6 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   n = 3\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substitute \\(n\\) back into the set:**\n   Now that we know \\(n = 3\\), we can substitute this value back into the original set to find the actual values of the set:\n   \\[\n   \\{3, 3 + 5, 3 + 6, 3 + 9, 3 + 15\\} = \\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the mean of the set:**\n   The mean is the sum of the elements divided by the number of elements. First, find the sum of the elements:\n   \\[\n   3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18 = 50\n   \\]\n   There are 5 elements in the set, so the mean is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Mean} = \\frac{50}{5} = 10\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the mean of the set is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
