      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve for the mean of the set \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\) given that its median is 9, we'll start by finding the median of the set and then use the given information to determine the value of \\(n\\).\n\n1. **Identify the median:**\n   The median of a sorted set of 5 elements is the 3rd element. According to the problem, the median is 9. So, we know:\n   \\[\n   n + 6 = 9\n   \\]\n\n2. **Solve for \\(n\\):**\n   \\[\n   n + 6 = 9\n   \\]\n   Subtract 6 from both sides to isolate \\(n\\):\n   \\[\n   n = 9 - 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n = 3\n   \\]\n\n3. **Form the set with \\(n = 3\\):**\n   Substituting \\(n = 3\\) into the original set:\n   \\[\n   \\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the mean:**\n   The mean of a set is the sum of its elements divided by the number of elements. Here, the set has 5 elements, so we first find the sum of the elements:\n   \\[\n   3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18\n   \\]\n   \n   Adding these step-by-step:\n   \\[\n   3 + 8 = 11\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   11 + 9 = 20\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   20 + 12 = 32\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   32 + 18 = 50\n   \\]\n   \n   The sum of the elements is 50. Now, divide by the number of elements (5):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Mean} = \\frac{50}{5} = 10\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]",
